**Filter Name:** `HL7 Escape Sequences and Special Characters Handler`

**Description:** This filter is designed to handle HL7 escape sequences and special characters in the Patient Identifier List (PID) segment, specifically in field 11 (Patient Name).

**Why do we need this?**

In healthcare integration, it's common for HL7 messages to contain special characters or escape sequences that can cause issues when processing or storing data. The PID-11 field is particularly sensitive because it contains patient name information, which should be accurately represented and stored.

**How does the filter work?**

The filter uses a combination of regular expressions (regex) and string manipulation techniques to identify and handle HL7 escape sequences and special characters in the PID-11 field.

1. **Regex Pattern:** The filter uses the following regex pattern to match HL7 escape sequences: `\xHH` (where HH is a hexadecimal value). This pattern matches any character that starts with an ASCII escape sequence (`\x`) followed by two hexadecimal digits.
2. **String Manipulation:** Once an escape sequence is identified, the filter replaces it with its corresponding ASCII character using the `Character.getNumericValue()` method.

**Filter Code:**
```xml
<filter>
  <name>HL7 Escape Sequences and Special Characters Handler</name>
  <description>This filter handles HL7 escape sequences and special characters in PID.11 fields.</description>

  <!-- Regex pattern to match HL7 escape sequences -->
  <regex-pattern>\xHH</regex-pattern>

  <!-- String manipulation code -->
  <script>
    <![CDATA[
      // Get the PID-11 field value
      String pid11Value = message.get('PID.11');

      // Replace any escape sequences with their corresponding ASCII character
      pid11Value = pid11Value.replace(/(\xHH)/g, (match) => {
        int hexValue = Character.getNumericValue(match);
        return Character.toChars(hexValue);
      });

      // Update the PID-11 field value in the message
      message.put('PID.11', pid11Value);
    ]]>
  </script>

</filter>
```
