Here's an example of how you can implement the FHIR R4 update operation on Goal resources using Java:

```java
import ca.uhn.fhir.context.FhirContext;
import ca.uhn.fhir.model.api.BundleFactory;
import ca.uhn.fhir.model.dstu2.resource.Goal;
import ca.uhn.fhir.rest.client.api.IGenericClient;

public class UpdateGoalResource {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a FHIR client
        IGenericClient client = FhirContext.forDstu3().newRestfulClient();

        // Set the base URL of your FHIR server
        String baseUrl = "https://your-fhir-server.com";

        // Create a Goal resource to update
        Goal goal = new Goal();
        goal.setId("example");
        goal.setCategory(new CodeableConcept().setText("Exercise"));
        goal.setCode(new Coding().setSystem("http://snomed.info/sct").setCode("123456"));

        // Set the patient ID for this goal
        String patientId = "patient-123";

        // Create a Bundle to hold our update request
        BundleFactory factory = FhirContext.forDstu3().newBundleFactory();
        Bundle bundle = factory.newBundle();

        // Add the Goal resource to the Bundle
        bundle.addEntry(factory.newEntry()
                .setResource(goal)
                .setRequest(new Request().setMethod(Request.Method.PUT))
                .setUrl(baseUrl + "/Goal/" + goal.getId()));

        // Set the patient ID in the Bundle's request URL
        bundle.getRequest().getUrl().append("/patient-" + patientId);

        // Execute the update request
        client.transaction().withBundle(bundle).execute();

        System.out.println("Goal resource updated successfully!");
    }
}
```

This code creates a FHIR client, sets up a Goal resource to update, and then uses the `IGenericClient` class from HAPI (the official Java library for interacting with FHIR servers) to send an HTTP PUT request to the FHIR server. The Bundle is used to group together multiple resources in a single transaction.

