class LRUCache {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.cacheMap = new Map();
    this.lruOrder = [];
  }

  get(key, defaultValue = undefined) {
    if (!this.cacheMap.has(key)) return defaultValue;
    const item = this.cacheMap.get(key);
    // Move the item to the end of the lruOrder array
    this.lruOrder.splice(this.lruOrder.indexOf(item), 1);
    this.lruOrder.push(item);
    return item.value;
  }

  set(key, value) {
    if (this.cacheMap.has(key)) {
      const oldItem = this.cacheMap.get(key);
      // Move the old item to the end of the lruOrder array
      this.lruOrder.splice(this.lruOrder.indexOf(oldItem), 1);
      this.lruOrder.push(oldItem);
    }
    const newItem = { key, value };
    this.cacheMap.set(key, newItem);
    this.lruOrder.push(newItem);
  }

  evictLRU() {
    if (this.capacity > 0) {
      const lruItem = this.lruOrder.shift();
      console.log(`Evicted ${lruItem.key} with value ${lruItem.value}`);
    }
  }
}
```

This implementation uses a `Map` to store the cache items and an array (`lruOrder`) to keep track of the order in which items were accessed. The `get()` method returns the value associated with a given key, moving it to the end of the `lruOrder` array if it exists. The `set()` method sets a new item or updates an existing one, and moves the old item (if it existed) to the end of the `lruOrder` array. The `evictLRU()` method removes the least recently used item from the cache.

Note that this implementation has O(log n) complexity for insertion and deletion operations, as it uses binary search on the `lruOrder` array to find the position where an item should be inserted or removed.

