Here's an example of implementing Dijkstra's algorithm in Java:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class Dijkstra {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a graph with 4 nodes (A, B, C, D)
        List<List<Integer>> graph = new ArrayList<>();
        graph.add(new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(0, 1)));
        graph.add(new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(1, 2)));
        graph.add(new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(2, 3)));
        graph.add(new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(3, 4)));

        // Create a priority queue
        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();

        // Initialize distances to all nodes as infinity
        int[] dist = {Integer.MAX_VALUE, Integer.MAX_VALUE, Integer.MAX_VALUE, Integer.MAX_VALUE};

        // Set the distance from A to itself as 0
        dist[0] = 0;

        // Add node A with distance 0 to the priority queue
        pq.add(0);

        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            int current = pq.poll();
            if (current != 0) { // If we've reached node D, stop
                break;
            }

            for (int neighbor : graph.get(current)) {
                if (dist[neighbor] > dist[current] + 1) { // Update distance if a shorter path is found
                    dist[neighbor] = dist[current] + 1;
                    pq.add(neighbor);
                }
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Shortest distances from A to all nodes:");
        for (int i : dist) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

This code creates a graph with 4 nodes and initializes the distances between them. It then uses Dijkstra's algorithm to find the shortest path from node A to all other nodes, using a priority queue to always expand the node with the smallest distance.

Note that this implementation has O(n^2) complexity because it iterates over each edge in the graph for each node processed.

