```javascript
function mergeSort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;

  let mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  let left = mergeSort(arr.slice(0, mid));
  let right = mergeSort(arr.slice(mid));

  return [...left, ...right].sort((a, b) => a - b);
}
```

This implementation uses the divide-and-conquer approach to sort an array. The `mergeSort` function recursively splits the input array into two halves until each half has only one element or less. Then it merges these sorted sub-arrays back together.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n log n), where n is the length of the input array. This is because each recursive call reduces the problem size by a factor of 2, and there are log n such calls before the base case (when the array has only one element) is reached.

