```typescript
function binarySearch(arr: number[], target: number): number | null {
  let low = 0;
  let high = arr.length - 1;

  while (low <= high) {
    const midIndex = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);
    if (arr[midIndex] === target) {
      return arr[midIndex];
    } else if (target < arr[midIndex]) {
      high = midIndex - 1;
    } else {
      low = midIndex + 1;
    }
  }

  return null; // or throw an error, depending on your use case
}
```

This function takes a sorted array and a target value as input. It returns the target value if it's found in the array, or `null` if not.

The time complexity of this implementation is O(log n), where n is the length of the input array. This is because each iteration of the loop reduces the search space by half, on average.

