Here's an example of how to implement Breadth-First Search (BFS) algorithm using a queue data structure:

```typescript
interface Node {
  value: any;
  children?: Node[];
}

function bfs(rootNode: Node): Node[] {
  const result = [];
  const queue = [rootNode];

  while (queue.length > 0) {
    const current = queue.shift();

    if (current.children && current.children.length > 0) {
      for (const child of current.children) {
        queue.push(child);
      }
    }

    result.push(current.value);
  }

  return result;
}
```

In this implementation, we define a `Node` interface to represent each node in the tree. The `bfs` function takes a root node as input and returns an array of values representing the breadth-first traversal of the tree.

The algorithm works by maintaining a queue that holds nodes to be processed. We start with the root node added to the queue. Then, we repeatedly dequeue a node from the front of the queue, process its value (add it to our result array), and enqueue all its child nodes if they exist. This ensures that each level of the tree is fully traversed before moving on to the next level.

The time complexity of this implementation is O(n log n) because for each node, we perform a constant amount of work and enqueue its children, which takes logarithmic time in the number of nodes due to the queue operations.

