```java
public class MergeSort {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {4, 2, 7, 1, 3};
        System.out.println("Original array: " + Arrays.toString(arr));

        // Sort the array using merge sort
        mergeSort(arr);

        System.out.println("Sorted array: " + Arrays.toString(arr));
    }

    public static void mergeSort(int[] arr) {
        if (arr.length <= 1) {
            return; // Base case: If the array has one or zero elements, it's already sorted.
        }

        int mid = arr.length / 2;
        int leftHalf[] = Arrays.copyOfRange(arr, 0, mid);
        int rightHalf[] = Arrays.copyOfRange(arr, mid, arr.length);

        // Recursively sort the two halves
        mergeSort(leftHalf);
        mergeSort(rightHalf);

        // Merge the sorted halves into a single array
        merge(leftHalf, rightHalf, arr);
    }

    public static void merge(int[] leftHalf, int[] rightHalf, int[] arr) {
        int i = 0; // Index for the left half
        int j = 0; // Index for the right half

        while (i < leftHalf.length && j < rightHalf.length) {
            if (leftHalf[i] <= rightHalf[j]) {
                arr[i + j] = leftHalf[i];
                i++;
            } else {
                arr[i + j] = rightHalf[j];
                j++;
            }
        }

        // Copy the remaining elements from either half
        System.arraycopy(leftHalf, i, arr, i + j, leftHalf.length - i);
        System.arraycopy(rightHalf, j, arr, i + j + (rightHalf.length - j), rightHalf.length - j);
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a recursive approach to sort the array. The `mergeSort` method divides the input array into two halves until each half has one or zero elements. Then it recursively sorts the two halves and merges them back together using the `merge` method.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(log n), where n is the number of elements in the input array, because the recursion depth decreases by a factor of 2 at each step.

