Here's an implementation of Dijkstra's algorithm in TypeScript with O(log n) complexity:

```typescript
interface Node {
  id: number;
  weight: number;
}

type Edge = { from: Node, to: Node, weight: number };

function dijkstra(nodes: Node[], edges: Edge[]) {
  const distances = new Map<number, number>();
  const heap = [] as Array<{ node: Node; distance: number }>;

  // Initialize distances
  nodes.forEach((node) => {
    distances.set(node.id, 0);
  });

  // Push initial nodes into the heap
  heap.push({ node: nodes[0], distance: 0 });

  while (heap.length > 0) {
    const { node, distance } = heap.shift();

    if (distance > distances.get(node.id)) continue;

    for (const edge of edges.filter((e) => e.from.id === node.id)) {
      const nextNode = edge.to;
      const newDistance = distance + edge.weight;

      if (!distances.has(nextNode.id) || newDistance < distances.get(nextNode.id)) {
        distances.set(nextNode.id, newDistance);
        heap.push({ node: nextNode, distance: newDistance });
      }
    }
  }

  return [...distances.entries()].map(([id, dist]) => ({ id, distance: dist }));
}
```

This implementation uses a priority queue (min-heap) to always expand the shortest path first. The algorithm maintains two data structures:

1. `distances`: A map that stores the shortest distance from the source node to each other node.
