Here's an example of how to implement Dijkstra's algorithm using a priority queue (min-heap) and adjacency list representation:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class Dijkstra {
    private int[][] graph; // adjacency matrix or list
    private int n; // number of nodes

    public Dijkstra(int[][] graph, int n) {
        this.graph = graph;
        this.n = n;
    }

    public void dijkstra() {
        int[] dist = new int[n];
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            dist[i] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
            visited[i] = false;
        }

        // Priority queue (min-heap)
        java.util.PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new java.util.ArrayList<>();

        // Initialize the priority queue with all nodes
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int[] node = {i, dist[i]};
            pq.add(node);
        }

        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            int[] current = pq.poll();

            if (visited[current[0]]) continue;

            // Update the distance to this node
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (graph[current[0]][j] != 0) { // edge exists
                    if (!visited[j]) {
                        int newDistance = current[1] + graph[current[0]][j];
                        if (newDistance < dist[j]) {
                            dist[j] = newDistance;
                            visited[j] = true;
                            pq.add(new java.util.ArrayList<int[]>());
                            pq.add({j, newDistance});
                        }
                    }
                }
            }

            // Mark this node as visited
            visited[current[0]] = true;
        }

        System.out.println("Shortest distances from node 0:");
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            System.out.print(dist[i] + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a priority queue to always expand the shortest path first. The algorithm iterates until all nodes are visited, and then prints the shortest distances from node 0.

Note that this is an O(n log n) complexity implementation because of the heap operations. If you want to achieve O(n), you can use a Fibonacci heap or a priority queue with a more efficient data structure.

